scope: |
  Measure the v2 runtime (VM + JVM-bytecode lane) against the v1 interpreter on the
  wall-clock corpus, then close the largest gaps in the v2 VM.

  FIRST FINDING (2026-07-28): the v2 bench lane is DEAD and reports `n/a`, not a failure.
  `ssc bench --backend v2|v2-bytecode` emits nothing and exits 0 because the generated
  wrapper calls `System.nanoTime()`, which the v2 native lane turns into an unhandled
  runtime effect (`Runtime.methodOp` → `PortableEffects.perform("System.nanoTime")`).
  So NOBODY has measured v2 runtime perf since the bench lane moved to the native front.
  Fix the apparatus first (os-plugin registers System.nanoTime/currentTimeMillis, the
  same tag-qualified-global pattern as Random.uuid), then measure, then optimise.

  Path widening (2026-07-28): +v2/runtime/std/os-plugin and the conformance case for it.

  SECOND FINDING (2026-07-28, re-verified independently with javap on
  v2/src/target/scala-3.8.3/classes): the v2 kernel has exactly ONE method over
  HotSpot's HugeMethodLimit — `ssc.Prims$.resolveBuiltinRaw$$anonfun$149`, the
  `__method__` dispatch match, at **49,384 bytecodes** (6.2x the 8000 limit). With
  -XX:-DontCompileHugeMethods on by default that method is NEVER JIT-compiled, so every
  non-arithmetic operation in every v2 program runs in the bytecode interpreter. Next
  largest in the kernel is arithRest at 5,235; `ssc.bytecode.JvmByteGen$.gen` is at
  7,292 (91% of the limit) and is the next one to breach.

  Resumed 2026-07-28T03:35Z after the previous session ended mid-slice (heartbeat was
  57 min stale, no live process, uncommitted split in the worktree). Same agent id.

